Upstream-Name: binwalk
Upstream-Contact: Craig Heffner <heffnercj@gmail.com>
Source: https://github.com/ReFirmLabs/binwalk

Files: *
Copyright: 2024 Craig Heffner <heffnercj@gmail.com>
License: MIT
Comment:
 The upstream LICENSE file reads "Copyright (c) 2024 devttys0"; devttys0 is
 Craig Heffner's GitHub account (same e-mail address in the upstream Git
 history). Every file in the 3.1.0 crate was written or rewritten in 2024 for
 the Rust rewrite of binwalk; files that existed in the earlier Python
 implementation (README.md, Dockerfile, .gitignore, .dockerignore, LICENSE)
 share no substantive content with their older versions. A few other people
 contributed small changes upstream under the same license.
 .
 images/binwalk_animated.svg is a termtosvg recording of binwalk scanning a
 firmware image. The "Copyright (C) 1998-2008 Erik Andersen, Rob Landley, Denys
 Vlasenko and others. Licensed under GPLv2." string in it is part of the
 displayed scan output (a BusyBox banner found inside the scanned firmware),
 not a notice that applies to the SVG file. The font is referenced by name only
 and is not embedded.
 .
 scripts/binwalk-ui is excluded from the Debian source (debcargo.toml
 excludes). It is adapted from a CC BY-SA 3.0 answer by Stephane Chazelas on
 Unix & Linux Stack Exchange (2016) and is not needed by the packaged crate.

Files: src/extractors/autel.rs
Copyright: 2024 Craig Heffner <heffnercj@gmail.com>
License: MIT
Comment:
 The Autel firmware de-obfuscation routine is a Rust reimplementation of the
 algorithm described in
 https://gist.github.com/sector7-nl/3fc815cd2497817ad461bfbd393294cb (a Python
 script, which carries no license or copyright notice). The byte lookup table
 is functional data required to decode the firmware format.

